.TH MACHO\-FINDLITERAL 1 "October 2, 2026" "MachO-explorer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
macho\-findliteral \- find a byte string inside a Mach\-O file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macho\-findliteral
.I literal
.RI [ binary | bundle ]
.RB [ \-\-text ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-b | \-\-binary
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Searches the whole file, or its
.B __TEXT
section with
.BR \-\-text ,
for a literal byte string, and reports each occurrence with its file offset,
its virtual address where that offset is mapped, and surrounding printable
context.
.PP
The literal is matched as raw bytes, so C\-style escapes work and a pattern
containing shell metacharacters needs no quoting beyond the shell's own. This
is the tool to reach for when a name is a format magic rather than a symbol: it
reads bytes, so it still works on a stripped binary.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-binary " " \fIpath\fR
The binary to read.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON object to standard output and send prose to standard error.
Offsets are rendered as
.B "0x..."
strings where they are 64\-bit.
.TP
.B \-\-text
Restrict the search to the
.B __TEXT
section, which is much cheaper when the literal is already known to be there.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACHO_BINARY
The binary to read when none is given on the command line.
.TP
.B MACHO_APP
An application bundle to read, tried after
.BR MACHO_BINARY .
.TP
.B MACHO_CONFIG
Path to a replacement
.IR config.json ,
including the scan chunk size and overlap, which are performance knobs and never
change what a search finds.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one occurrence was found.
.TP
.B 1
The tool ran and the literal was not present. A distinct negative result, not an
error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, including a missing literal.
.TP
.B 3
The file could not be read.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
macho\-findliteral LZ4 "/Applications/Some App.app"
macho\-findliteral \\x1f\\x8b \-\-text /usr/bin/ssh
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
An occurrence in a slice other than the one whose address is shown is possible
when a fat binary contains the same literal twice. Every hit carries the slice
it was found in.
.PP
To go from an occurrence to the code that handles it, map the literal to its
addresses with
.BR macho\-mapliteral (1),
which also searches for pointers to those addresses.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR macho\-mapliteral (1),
.BR macho\-findcall (1),
.BR macho\-describe (1),
.BR strings (1)